<!--
The wasm example home page, booting `beet-min.wasm`: the smallest binary that is
still a beet runtime in the browser, so its program (`hello.bsx`) sticks to the core
`<Sequence>`/`<Log>`/`CallOnReady` vocabulary. A full `<HtmlDocument>` so the route
renders the whole document directly (no layout middleware): `<Stylesheet>` +
`<ColorSchemeScript>` + `<LiveReloadScript>` in the head, the console panel and the
wasm loader in the body.

`<Wasm src repo main>` boots the wasm `beet` binary with its launch: the served
repo store and the entry within it, which the binary resolves and runs headless.
`<RenderConsole>` mirrors its console output onto the page, and sits before `<Wasm>`
so its console wrap installs before the (deferred) module boots.
-->
<HtmlDocument>
	<!-- a `<Fragment slot="head">` forwards all its children into the head slot;
	     `<ColorSchemeScript>` seeds the light/dark class before paint and honors
	     ?color-scheme=dark. -->
	<Fragment slot="head">
		<Stylesheet/>
		<ColorSchemeScript/>
		<LiveReloadScript/>
	</Fragment>
	<h1>Beet in the browser</h1>
	<p>
		A headless beet program, compiled to wasm, running in your browser. Its
		console output renders below.
	</p>
	<p>
		This is the smallest binary that still runs a beet program: the render router,
		the BSX parser and the action runtime, and nothing else. The
		<a href="/scripting">scripting page</a> boots the other end of the range,
		<code>beet-full.wasm</code>, which carries every feature that compiles for the
		browser.
	</p>
	<RenderConsole/>
	<Wasm src="/assets/wasm/beet-min.wasm" repo="/examples/wasm" main="hello.bsx"/>
</HtmlDocument>
